==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{m|goh|sizzen}}, originally {{m|goh||*sitten}}, from {{etyl|gem-pro|de}} {{m|gem-pro|*sitjaną}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|de}} {{m|ine-pro|*sed-}}. Cognate with {{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|sitten}}, {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|zitten}}, {{etyl|en|-}} {{m|en|sit}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{m|da|sidde}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈzɪtsn̩/|/ˈzɪtsən/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-sitzen.ogg|audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=5|sitzt|saß|gesessen|past subjunctive=säße|auxiliary=both}}

# {{label|de|intransitive}} to [[sit]]; to [[perch]]
# {{label|de|intransitive}} to [[stay]] (in one place); to [[remain]]; to [[be]] (in a particular place or state)
# {{label|de|intransitive|of clothing}} to [[fit]]
# {{label|de|intransitive|in certain constructions|e.g. with ''{{l|de|voller}}''}} to [[be]]
#: ''Der Schrank '''sitzt''' voller Motten.'' — “The cupboard '''is''' full of moths.”
# {{label|de|intransitive|colloquial}} to [[do time]]; to [[spend]] time in jail
# {{label|de|intransitive|colloquial|of a strike, a comment, etc.}} to [[hit home]]; to have a significant effect

====Usage notes====
The most frequent auxiliary with ''sitzen'' is {{m|de|haben}}: ''Ich habe gesessen.'' In northern and central Germany, only this form is used. In southern Germany, Austria, and Switzerland, {{m|de|sein}} is common in the vernacular and also, alternatively, in standard usage: ''Ich bin gesessen.''

